      "writtenOn": "2021-09-23T02:16:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why are the ranges verified before the signature / cached hash? You should always verify things from the top down so you touch as little potentially-malicious data as possible (even if it\u0027s not a real exploit vector, it could still be formatted to waste time and cause a practical denial of service).",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6ad53b88_e5e4cecd",
        "filename": "common/ap_ro_integrity_check.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1280,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-23T04:57:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "verify_ranges() first reads the ranges values from AP flash, note that this function is shared by the old and new AP RO verification flows.\n\nThe ranges values are needed for GVD verification. I agree that we could read the ranges values first, then verify  GVD and then verify the ranges, but this would cause some code expansion for no particularly good reason.",
      "parentUuid": "bfd341e3_1caa1c62",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7db9b2ff_d228dc73",
        "filename": "common/ap_ro_integrity_check.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1280,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-24T02:10:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, I think you should split loading and verifying of the ranges. (In fact I think it would be best if you just load the whole structure including signature and everything into dynamically allocated storage in one go, and then verify and use parts of it as needed. That\u0027s how vboot does these kinds of things.) It\u0027s important not to start hashing ranges before they have been verified, that would be an easy denial-of-service vector for clogging up the flash with a bunch of fake GSCVD structures that all for the GSC to hash the whole flash again and again.",
      "parentUuid": "6ad53b88_e5e4cecd",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f576ae8_18168439",
        "filename": "common/ap_ro_integrity_check.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1280,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-24T19:47:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am sorry, but I respectfully disagree: the chance that gvd signature verification would fail is much lower, than the chance that the RO areas hash would not match. This is why it makes sense to check the hash first.\n\nUnless some other reviewer agrees that this order of operations change is necessary I would prefer to leave this as is.\n\nAlso, about \"would be best if you just load the whole structure\" - I think this is where the root of our disagreements is: there is not enough memory on Cr50 heap to allocate a buffer which would fit the fool RO_GSCVD section.\n\nThe elements (the keys and the signature) must be loaded no more than two at a time.",
      "parentUuid": "7db9b2ff_d228dc73",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
