        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-29T00:08:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I am sorry, but I respectfully disagree: the chance that gvd signature verification would fail is much lower, than the chance that the RO areas hash would not match. This is why it makes sense to check the hash first.\n\nI don\u0027t understand what you are trying to argue for here? Efficiency? In the common case where verification succeeds, none of this matters. In the case where either of these are accidentally broken, the system refuses to boot anyway, there is no point in saving a couple of milliseconds when getting to that result. The only other situation where speed matters is when someone maliciously inserts bad descriptors in places on the RW flash that get parsed before the real descriptor, trying to get this code to waste minutes of time so the device practically appears dead on booting. In order to curtail that, it is imperative that we verify the range table before using it to check hashes, because otherwise it could just be written to always cover the whole flash for every fake entry.\n\nBesides, this is just basic security-conscious design: always verify data before using it. Verifying the range table first reduces the attack surface because an attacker can no longer supply arbitrary ranges to your range parsing code to try to exploit some mistake that we may have missed.",
      "parentUuid": "8f846507_2f99c0fc",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e52032a_47cccd1c",
        "filename": "common/ap_ro_integrity_check.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1280,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-29T00:13:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) yes, we can\u0027t have the entire GVD in memory in cr50 case.\n2) I don\u0027t see it as a major security concern if we check hashes first. yes, this would allow attackers to slow down cr50 checks, but we generally don\u0027t guard against DoS attacks if the attackers can access storage - they could just erase RW or OS and get a more reliable DoS that way. however, I believe we can avoid even such not very interesting attacks by checking the signature first.\n\nhow about we extract the read_signature_header .. verify_keyblock chain into a separate function and call it first. and I believe it only needs to be called if descriptor \u003d\u003d NULL, otherwise, gvd_cache_check is sufficient. so, the function would be even better:\n\nread_gscvd_header()\nif (descriptor) {\n  gvd_cache_check() and return -1 if doesn\u0027t match\n} else {\n  gvd_signature_check() and return -1 if doesn\u0027t match\n}\nverify_ranges() and return -1 if fail\nif (!descriptor) {\n  save_gvd_hash()\n}\n\nthis way we don\u0027t have a perf hit each time we do AP RO verification, but only if gvd_cache_check() doesn\u0027t match, and in that case the hashes better be good, otherwise the whole verification will fail. so it doesn\u0027t matter much if we spend some extra time to verify the signature first if it fails. and if the hashes verification succeeds we will ned to verify the GVD signature anyways.",
      "parentUuid": "8f846507_2f99c0fc",
      "revId": "e272a15aab408f1d370ed0383f3db203106dabe0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
